Humans have about the same number of hairs.\nSo 117,000 * 220 = 25,740,000.\nI will output this number, explain it briefly.</think>25,740,000\n\nHere is the justification for this estimate:\n\n1. **Total Giraffe Population:** Recent estimates by the Giraffe Conservation Foundation (GCF) and the IUCN place the total global wild giraffe population at roughly 117,000 individuals (with a few hundred more in captivity). I have used 117,000 for the calculation.\n2. **Spots per Giraffe:** Giraffes do not generally add new spots as they grow from infancy to adulthood; instead, their existing spots simply expand in size to cover their growing surface area (similar to how human skin cells stretch and grow). While the number of spots varies by species—Reticulated giraffes tend to have fewer, larger, more uniform polygonal spots (around 150-200), whereas Masai giraffes have smaller, more jagged and fragmented spots (often over 250)—a population-wide average across all four currently recognized species is approximately 220 spots per giraffe.\n\nMultiplying the estimated population (117,000) by the estimated average spots per giraffe (220) yields exactly 25,740,000.",
